# All variables are REQUIRED at boot (fail-fast) unless marked optional.
# Copy to server/.env for local development — never commit real values.

# PostgreSQL connection string.
# Local Docker: docker run -d -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=dev -e POSTGRES_DB=whatsapp_clickers -p 5432:5432 postgres:17
DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:dev@localhost:5432/whatsapp_clickers

# Meta WhatsApp Cloud API. From Story 2.1 onward the server refuses to boot
# with the placeholder "dummy"/"change-me..." values below — real values are
# required for `make dev` / `go run ./cmd/server`. See the README "Meta
# WhatsApp Business setup" section for the full runbook. Where each value
# lives in the Meta console:
#   WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN    - App dashboard -> WhatsApp -> API Setup ->
#                              temporary token (~24h, regenerate per dev
#                              session) or a System User permanent token
#                              (Business Settings -> System users) before
#                              the pilot.
#   WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID - App dashboard -> WhatsApp -> API Setup ->
#                              "Phone number ID" (the ID, never the display
#                              phone number).
#   WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET      - App dashboard -> App settings -> Basic ->
#                              App secret (used for X-Hub-Signature-256 HMAC
#                              verification of inbound webhooks).
#   WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN    - Self-chosen random string, e.g.
#                              `openssl rand -hex 16`; you invent this and
#                              enter the same value in the Meta webhook
#                              config's "Verify Token" field.
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=dummy
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=dummy
WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=dummy
WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=dummy

# The human-readable number the lobby page shows the Organizer to relay to
# the room. From Meta's Graph API: GET /{phone-number-id} -> the
# display_phone_number field (App dashboard -> WhatsApp -> API Setup shows
# it too, e.g. "+972 50-000-0000"). Fetched once and hardcoded here — no
# runtime Graph API call needed.
WHATSAPP_DISPLAY_NUMBER=dummy

# Optional — overrides the Meta Cloud API base URL the outbound client posts
# to. Unset in production and in normal local development: the client's pinned
# default (Graph API v25.0) is what you want. It exists so an end-to-end test
# harness can point the server at a local fake provider and assert that replies
# are really sent, instead of firing real WhatsApp messages on every run.
# Not required, not placeholder-validated.
#WHATSAPP_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9099/v25.0

# Anthropic API key for AI semantic answer validation. Epic 3 (story 3.6)
# is the first consumer — the placeholder below is NOT rejected at boot
# until then (validating an unconsumed secret would break every earlier
# boot for zero safety gain).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=dummy

# Secret for signing session tokens (HMAC). Must be >= 32 chars and NOT start
# with "change-me" — the server refuses to boot otherwise. Generate a real one:
#   Git Bash / Linux:  openssl rand -base64 48
#   PowerShell:        [Convert]::ToBase64String((1..48 | %{Get-Random -Max 256}))
SESSION_SECRET=change-me-long-random-string

# Optional — HTTP port, defaults to 8080. Railway injects this automatically.
#PORT=8080

# OPS NOTE — not read by the app, set on the RAILWAY SERVICE itself:
#   RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_DRAINING_SECONDS=30
# Railway's default grace between SIGTERM and SIGKILL is ZERO seconds, so
# without this variable the graceful-shutdown sequence in cmd/server/main.go
# (15s HTTP drain + 5s dispatcher drain + 2s stop grace, 22s worst case)
# never runs on a redeploy — queued replies are killed mid-drain and the
# chat goes silent on every merge to main. 30s covers the worst case with
# margin. Set it once in the Railway dashboard (service -> Variables).
